#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084516 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the rational equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{x}=\\dfrac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhen the equation consists of a single rational expression on each side, like the one above, then the easiest way to solve it is usually to use cross-multiplication.\n\nBefore we start, though, we note that $x=0$ *cannot* be a solution. This is because when $x=0$, we get a zero in the denominator on the left-hand side.\n\nNow, let's solve the equation. First, we cross-multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{x} & =\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 2\\cdot 5 & =x\\cdot 3 \\\\ 10 & =3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can solve for $x$ using the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =3x \\\\ \\frac{10}{3} & =\\frac{3x}{3} \\\\ \\frac{10}{3} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x= \\dfrac{10}{3}$ is different from $x=0$, we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is $x = \\dfrac{10}{3}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "89ea4830-364e-5105-8df0-54cf9f7e1e36" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8779 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084517 :tutorial/content "If there is a negative sign in a rational equation, we can still solve it using cross-multiplication. The only difference is that we have to move the negative sign onto the numerator of the fraction first.\n\nFor example, let's solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{2x} = -\\dfrac 9 8 .\n$$\n\nThis is similar to the equation from the previous example, but this time, there is a negative sign on the right-hand side.\n\nWe deal with the negative sign by moving it onto the numerator of the fraction, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{2x} = \\dfrac{\\color{red}-9}{8} .\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed to solve the equation by cross-multiplication, as usual. (We also note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined.) Cross-multiplying, we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac{3}{2x} &= \\dfrac{\\color{red}-9}{8} \\[5pt]\n\t\t3 \\cdot 8 &= 2x \\cdot (-9) \\[5pt]\n\t\t24 &= -18x\n\t}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t24 &= -18x \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac{24}{-18} &= \\dfrac{-18x}{-18} \\[5pt]\n\t\t-\\dfrac{4}{3} &= x\n\t}\n$$\n\nSince  $x = -\\dfrac{4}{3}$ is different from $x=0,$ we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is $x=-\\dfrac{4}{3}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d61fcf8b-f639-5c4c-8997-66813230bedf" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8780 :tutorial/title "Solving Rational Equations Containing Negative Signs"}]}
